              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 796,
                "end_index": 895,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Whispering (Unvoiced Phonation)\nWhen you whisper, your brain shifts to an \"unvoiced\" speech pattern"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 898,
                "end_index": 1138,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The \"Inverted Y\" Glottis:** Rather than bringing the vocal folds tightly together to vibrate, your laryngeal muscles (specifically the lateral cricoarytenoid and thyroarytenoid muscles) adjust the vocal folds into a unique configuration"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1140,
                "end_index": 1286,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The front and middle portions of your vocal folds are pressed together, but the back portion\u2014between the **arytenoid cartilages**\u2014remains open"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1288,
                "end_index": 1382,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This creates a small, triangular opening at the back, often described as an \"inverted Y\" shape"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1384,
                "end_index": 1547,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Creating Turbulence Instead of Vibration:** Because the vocal folds are held rigid and slightly apart, the air exhaled from your lungs cannot make them vibrate"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1549,
                "end_index": 1627,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Instead, the air is forced through the narrow \"inverted Y\" gap at a high speed"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1629,
                "end_index": 1678,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This creates **turbulent airflow** (air friction)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7,
                0
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1681,
                "end_index": 1859,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Aperiodic Noise:** Instead of a clean, vibrating pitch, this turbulence creates a broad spectrum of randomized frequencies, which we hear as a high-pitched \"hiss\" or \"static\""
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                2
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1862,
                "end_index": 2012,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Articulators Do the Rest:** Just like in normal speech, your tongue, teeth, lips, and palate shape this turbulent hiss into recognizable syllables"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2014,
                "end_index": 2212,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because there is no vocal fold vibration, all of your whispered vowels and consonants are unvoiced (for example, a whispered \"V\" naturally sounds like an \"F\", and a whispered \"Z\" sounds like an \"S\")"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2227,
                "end_index": 2384,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The Paradox: Why Whispering Strains Your Voice\nMany people whisper when they have laryngitis or a sore throat because they think it \"rests\" their vocal cords"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2386,
                "end_index": 2498,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Biologically, however, whispering is often **more taxing** on your larynx than speaking softly in a normal voice"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                11
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2631,
                "end_index": 2743,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "To restrict the airflow and keep the vocal folds from vibrating, you must tightly squeeze your laryngeal muscles"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                6,
                12
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2745,
                "end_index": 2894,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This frequently engages the *false vocal folds* (muscle structures located just above your true vocal folds), leading to throat squeezing and tension"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                10,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2896,
                "end_index": 3066,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Dryness and Friction:** The rapid, turbulent rush of air passing over the open glottis dries out the mucosal lining of your vocal cords much faster than normal speech"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
